From f5ebb382e8df71879ae1d493e9d7ed46be349bd3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 02:45:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: what the review of the display watch found

The watch of the displays that are connected after Kodi has started
(CDRMDisplayWatch) was read by an independent reviewer before it was
ever built. No deadlock or crash was found; what was found is in what
counts as an answer, in what keeps out of the way of playback, and in
the starts that do not look at every device.

What counts as an answer. The question closed as "move" whenever it was
closed by anything but a button or Back: by its time running out, as
meant, but also by whatever closes dialogs (a builtin, leaving the
fullscreen video). And put while Kodi's one yes/no dialog was open, it
wrote itself over the question that was there, whose answer was then
taken for its own.
- To move is now the button that says yes: only a press of it sets the
  dialog as confirmed. Everything else is "stay", but the dialog's own
  time running out, which the dialog tells apart (it is reset before
  the question, nothing else puts that mark back).
- The question is not put while the dialog is open, while any dialog
  waits for the user, or while a video plays; and an answer counts only
  if the dialog still has the heading of this question and did not
  close sooner than a person answers.
- With nobody answering, Kodi moves only if nothing plays and no dialog
  waits at that moment, and it is started again unasked twice at most
  in a run of the system, once for an output (a note in /run): a
  display that comes and goes cannot keep Kodi starting. With no output
  at all (nobody to ask) the same holds, and Kodi waits for whatever
  plays to end.
- The question, the checks around it and the order to start again are
  one piece of work of the application thread: nothing is decided
  later, by another thread, on what was true before.
- "Stay" is forgotten when the display is unplugged: plugged in again,
  it is offered again. The watch waits for a question that is being
  shown as long as it is shown (moving in it starts its time anew).

Out of the way of playback. Whether a video played was asked once,
before the outputs were looked at, and the 10 s after a video were
counted from that answer. The renderers now tell the watch whenever a
video output is set up or taken down (SetVideoOutput(), SetHDR()), and
nothing is looked at for 10 s after the last of those or of a video
seen playing; a dialog that waits (the one of a stream that is being
opened) holds the look back too.

Starts that do not look at every device. Where the first device that
has a display is taken (the chosen one could not be started), what the
start recorded was one device against the several of a later look, and
the output in use carried a name that no look gives it. A look now
carries the node of each device and whether it could be asked
(DRMLook): looks are compared only when they are at the same nodes; the
output in use is found by the node of its device and takes the name of
the look; a device that could not be asked keeps what was last known of
it, and nothing on it counts as new; and where the start did not look
at every device, the watch takes its own first look.

Also:
- no device is opened by the watch before the displays that Kodi does
  not use have been turned off (CDRMUtils::OtherDevicesPending()): a
  look that had such a device open at that moment kept Kodi from being
  its master, and its displays stayed lit;
- what the watch needs of Kodi is done by calls posted to the
  application thread, the same way as the question: the call made for
  every frame is gone, and so are the two flags it read. Where no other
  output could rank above the one in use the watch asks nothing at all
  (the Monitor setting tells it when it changes);
- each look is told in the log (the first twenty without debug
  logging), and reading the EDID of a display for a look no longer
  writes it out in the log each time (CDisplayInfo::Create(edid, false));
- anything thrown while looking ends the look, or the watch, with a
  line in the log.

Not done: an output that Kodi declines keeps the console of the kernel
on it when nothing of Kodi holds its device; and the notice of a laptop
that is on its own panel comes at every start (naming the panel in the
Monitor setting turns it off).

The decision is in DRMOutputChoice.h and tested on the host, now also
for looks at other devices, devices that could not be asked, the name
of the output in use and "stay" being forgotten (drmoutput_test.cpp).
The rest is read and compiled, and has not run on a graphics device.
